{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"═══ VERDICT PARITY — reported straight, because it is NOT clean. Surfaced findings are identical\non every run (5/5), but relation LABELS shift on 6 of 12 ordered pairs, reproducibly, and the\nshift is mostly toward the conservative end. ═══\n\n**THE CONTROL THAT MAKES THIS READABLE.** A single before/after comparison cannot separate \"the\njudge is noisy\" from \"batching changed the judge\", so each build was run TWICE on the same corpus\nand the within-build churn measured first.\n\n    WITHIN-build churn (identical code, two runs)   B vs B2 per-pair   1 / 12\n                                                    C vs C2 batched    3 / 12\n    BETWEEN-build churn                             A vs B  (8415)     0 / 12\n                                                    B vs C             6 / 12\n                                                    B2 vs C2           6 / 12   <- reproduces\n\n6/12 on two independent pairs of runs against a 1–3/12 noise floor: the shift is **systematic**,\nnot sampling variance. (A vs B at 0/12 also re-confirms 8415's own 12/12 parity claim.)\n\n    pair            A pre-8415        B 8415            B2 rerun          C batched         C2 rerun\n    2fd4#first      depends_on        depends_on        depends_on        related_distinct  related_distinct\n    2fd4#second     related_distinct  related_distinct  depends_on        related_distinct  depends_on\n    4b5b#first      related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct\n    4b5b#second     related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct\n    e68d#first      related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct\n    e68d#second     related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct\n    2610#first      related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  unrelated         unrelated\n    2610#second     related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  unrelated         unrelated\n    9249#first      related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  depends_on\n    9249#second     related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  depends_on        related_distinct\n    2bbd#first      related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  unrelated         unrelated\n    2bbd#second     related_distinct  related_distinct  related_distinct  unrelated         unrelated\n\n    surfaced findings   A []   B []   B2 []   C []   C2 []\n\n**WHAT THE SHIFT IS.** Five of the six divergences move AWAY from surfacing: `2610` and `2bbd`\n(four pairs) go `related_distinct` → `unrelated`, and `2fd4#first` goes `depends_on` →\n`related_distinct` — the batched judge declines a directional relation the per-pair judge asserted.\nSeeing five candidates side by side appears to make the model readier to call a weak neighbour\nplainly unrelated, which is the direction a prompt whose own rule is \"false flags are costly\"\nshould prefer.\n\nThe sixth, `9249`, is the one that moves the wrong way: the batched build asserts `depends_on` in\nONE ordering — `#second` in run C, `#first` in run C2, i.e. it does not even pick the same\nordering twice. It never survives aggregation, because the both-orderings-agree rule downgrades a\none-sided directional claim. This is precisely the case that rule exists for, and it held.\n\n**RECALL — the gap in the above, and the control that closes it.** All five runs surfaced `[]`, so\nthe corpus contains no true positive and CANNOT distinguish \"batching preserves recall\" from\n\"batching surfaces nothing\". A positive control was therefore run: the query's own digest injected\nas a seventh candidate (a guaranteed `duplicates`) alongside five real negatives, judged on both\nbuilds.\n\n    per-pair (B):  ZZZZ-self#first duplicates/1.0   #second duplicates/1.0\n    batched  (C):  ZZZZ-self#first duplicates/1.0   #second duplicates/1.0\n\n    finding, BOTH builds, byte-identical including the cited artifact:\n      rebar link 6a89-1165-ea58-4be3 ZZZZ-self-duplicate duplicates\n      shared_artifact: docs/adr/NNNN-opcert-asymmetric.md   confidence 1.0\n\n    tokens on that corpus:  per-pair 12 calls / 18,478 in  ->  batched 2 calls / 9,086 in\n\nSo the true positive is recovered at full confidence in both orderings, with the same cited\nartifact, and the five surrounding negatives did not contaminate it — the cross-contamination this\nchange risks did not occur on the one true positive available to test.\n\n**HONEST LIMITS.** One query ticket, six candidates, two runs per build, one synthetic positive.\nThat is enough to show the finding-level output is unchanged and the systematic relation shift is\nconservative; it is NOT enough to bound recall loss on real duplicates at scale. A corpus of real\nlabelled positives would be needed for that, and this repo's store did not supply one for the\nquery measured."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785713999469909001,"uuid":"65a8210a-7820-4afe-9c27-061fc7cd704d"}